"Separates accumulating quantities from the rates that change them and the information relationships governing those rates.\n\nUse when: Reason about backlogs, inventories, populations or other accumulations. Prepare a quantitative dynamic model with explicit units and boundaries.\n\nAvoid when: Only a qualitative causal story is needed and no accumulations matter.\n\nVisual grammar: Stock boxes represent quantities at an instant. Flow pipes and valves represent quantities per unit time. Information links influence rates; sources and sinks mark model boundaries.\n\nBuild: Choose the accumulations and state their initial values and units. Identify every relevant inflow and outflow with compatible rate units. Add the information dependencies governing rates and test boundary assumptions.\n\nCheck: Stock units equal flow units multiplied by time. Material flows and information links are visually distinct. No quantity disappears or appears without an explicit modeled flow.\n\nPitfalls: Treating a rate such as weekly arrivals as an accumulated stock. Using a qualitative diagram alone as evidence of numerical performance.\n\nExample: A repair backlog accumulates arriving jobs and decreases through completed repairs, with staffing and productivity influencing the completion rate.\n\nAlternatives: Causal-loop diagram, Value-stream map."
